1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:12,250
A student who memorises past papers word for word fails when the questions change. A model can do the same: perfect on training data, poor on new data.

2
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:18,700
Keep models simple enough, and always judge them on data they have never seen.

3
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:27,375
Ninety-nine percent on training data, but seventy percent on test data? The model memorised.

4
00:00:28,070 --> 00:00:35,870
Simpler models, more data, and limits on depth help. Regularisation gently punishes complexity.

5
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:44,605
Cross-validation rotates which part is the test, like several mock exams instead of one.

6
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:52,600
Check results for each group of people. A model can be accurate overall, and unfair to one group.

